gpu_decode_2026-09-17_rtx2080_pretuning.tsv

The "before" of the kernel tuning in commit 711233ad: `gpu/` exactly as it was at 417be423.
The "after" is gpu_decode_2026-09-17_rtx2080.tsv, same host, same day, same command.

adapter: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080 [Vulkan DiscreteGpu; NVIDIA 580.178.04] (proprietary driver,
  nouveau blacklisted; the box has no other discrete GPU). Vulkan instance 1.4.341,
  libvulkan1 1.4.341.0-1. 8 GiB, SM clock 1515 MHz and memory 7000 MHz under load
  (`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=clocks.sm,clocks.mem`), so the card was not stuck at idle clocks.
wgpu: 30.0.1
host: dev (Ryzen 9 9950X3D, 32 threads), Ubuntu, kernel 7.0.0-31-generic. **Shared with other
  agents while this ran**: see the noise note below.
command: RAYON_NUM_THREADS=8 nice -n 19 gpu_bench --adapter GeForce --rounds 7 --max-size 4096
  --out <this file>
commit: gpu/ contents of 417be423 (binary built from that tree); everything else of the working
  copy that became 711233ad.
columns: header comment of gpu/examples/gpu_bench.rs. cpu_mt_ms is the crate's CPU engine with
  RAYON_NUM_THREADS=8, not all 32 threads of the box, so it understates a full-box CPU run;
  cpu_1t_ms is one thread.

Noise: the box runs several agents. Medians over 7 interleaved rounds absorb most of it, but not
all: the Hop 384x384 row (78.73 ms device) is a clear outlier — it is 3.9x the 256x256 row where
the pixel count only grows 2.25x, and the same cell measures 42.93 ms in the "after" file, which
sits on the smooth curve. Treat single rows with suspicion; the curve shape is sound.
